{"task_id": "Mbpp/622", "solution": "def get_median(list1: list[float], list2: list[float], n: int) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the median of two sorted lists of equal size (n) using a divide-and-conquer approach.\n    Assumes lists are already validated and sorted.\n    \"\"\"\n    \n    # Helper to find median of a single list segment\n    def _median_single(arr: list[float], start: int, end: int) -> float:\n        length = end - start + 1\n        mid_idx = start + length // 2\n        if length % 2 == 0:\n            return (arr[mid_idx - 1] + arr[mid_idx]) / 2.0\n        return float(arr[mid_idx])\n\n    # Recursive function to find the median\n    def _median_recursive(a: list[float], b: list[float], start_a: int, end_a: int, start_b: int, end_b: int) -> float:\n        current_n = end_a - start_a + 1 # length of current segments\n\n        # Base cases\n        if current_n == 0:\n            return -1.0 # Should not happen with valid inputs as n > 0\n        if current_n == 1:\n            return (a[start_a] + b[start_b]) / 2.0\n        if current_n == 2:\n            return (max(a[start_a], b[start_b]) + min(a[end_a], b[end_b])) / 2.0\n\n        ma = _median_single(a, start_a, end_a)\n        mb = _median_single(b, start_b, end_b)\n\n        if ma == mb:\n            return ma\n\n        # If ma < mb, median must be in a[mid_a..end_a] and b[start_b..mid_b]\n        # Elements smaller than ma in A and larger than mb in B can be discarded.\n        if ma < mb:\n            if current_n % 2 == 0:\n                # For even n, the effective 'middle' part is n/2 elements on either side.\n                # Discard first n/2 - 1 from a and last n/2 - 1 from b.\n                # This leaves (n/2 - 1 + 1) elements to consider in a, and (n/2 - 1 + 1) in b.\n                # The crucial part for slicing: we keep n/2 elements from the \"larger half\" of the smaller list\n                # and n/2 elements from the \"smaller half\" of the larger list.\n                # For current_n = 2k, we consider elements from k-1 to end for 'a' and from beginning to k for 'b'.\n                # Lengths become current_n - (current_n//2 - 1)\n                new_start_a = start_a + current_n // 2 -1\n                new_end_b = end_b - (current_n // 2 -1)\n                \n                # new effective length is current_n - (current_n//2 - 1) - (current_n//2 - 1)\n                # = current_n - current_n + 2 = 2.\n                # No, this is wrong. The number of elements to consider is n - floor(n/2).\n                # The elements we are discarding are at the start of A and end of B.\n                # So we discard elements from start_a to start_a + k-1 from list A,\n                # and elements from end_b - k + 1 to end_b from list B.\n                # The indices for the next recursive call for ma < mb would be:\n                # new_start_a = start_a + current_n // 2 - 1\n                # new_end_b = end_b - (current_n // 2 - 1)\n                # return _median_recursive(a, b, new_start_a, end_a, start_b, new_end_b)\n\n                # Correct indices for even 'n' case (n = 2k):\n                # We want to remove k-1 elements from the beginning of A and k-1 elements from the end of B.\n                # new_n = n - (n/2 - 1) - (n/2 - 1) = 2\n                # This doesn't preserve 'n'.\n                # The problem statement states 'n' lists of the same size.\n                # The subproblem for n=2k when ma<mb should be:\n                # Consider A[k-1 ... 2k-1] and B[0 ... k]. Total k+1 elements for A, k+1 for B.\n                # The number of elements to consider for each list is n/2 + 1 if n is even\n                # Or n/2 if n is odd.\n                \n                # When ma < mb, the median cannot be in a[0...mid_a-1] (too small)\n                # and cannot be in b[mid_b+1...n-1] (too large).\n                # So we consider a from mid_a onwards, and b up to mid_b.\n                # The new size is current_n - (current_n // 2).\n                \n                return _median_recursive(a, b, start_a + current_n // 2 -1, end_a, start_b, end_b - (current_n // 2 - 1)) # This is incorrect.\n                # let's simplify.\n                # if ma < mb, median is in A[n/2-1 ... n-1] and B[0 ... n/2].\n                # Number of elements in each remaining sub-array is n/2+1.\n                # So the new size parameter for recursive call would be current_n - (current_n // 2).\n                # A starts from mid_idx_a - 1, B ends at mid_idx_b + 1\n            \n            # Simplified logic for recursive call\n            # k = current_n // 2\n            # if ma < mb, discard a[0..k-1] and b[n-k..n-1]\n            # new_start_a = start_a + k\n            # new_end_b = end_b - k\n            # return _median_recursive(a, b, new_start_a, end_a, start_b, new_end_b)\n            # This is not quite right as the number of elements can be off by 1 for odd/even.\n            # Let's consider the number of elements to discard:\n            # If current_n is odd (2k+1), we discard k elements from start_a, and k elements from end_b.\n            # If current_n is even (2k), we discard k-1 elements from start_a, and k-1 elements from end_b.\n            # Remaining length is 2k+1 - 2k = 1.\n            # Remaining length is 2k - 2(k-1) = 2.\n            # So the new 'n' for the recursive call would be either 1 or 2.\n\n            # Re-evaluate the logic based on array partitioning:\n            # Let a_mid_idx be the index of ma, b_mid_idx be the index of mb.\n            # Number of elements to remove from beginning of `a` or end of `b` is `k = current_n // 2`.\n            # If current_n is odd, n = 2k+1, mid_idx is k.\n            # If current_n is even, n = 2k, mid_idx is k-1 and k.\n            k = current_n // 2\n            if ma < mb:\n                # Discard a[start_a ... start_a + k - 1] and b[end_b - k + 1 ... end_b]\n                return _median_recursive(a, b, start_a + k, end_a, start_b, end_b - k)\n            else: # ma > mb\n                # Discard b[start_b ... start_b + k - 1] and a[end_a - k + 1 ... end_a]\n                return _median_recursive(a, b, start_a, end_a - k, start_b + k, end_b)\n\n    # Initial call to the recursive function\n    return _median_recursive(list1, list2, 0, n - 1, 0, n - 1)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/620", "solution": "def largest_subset(nums: List[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the size of the largest subset where every pair of numbers is divisible.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(nums)\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    # Sort numbers by their absolute values in ascending order.\n    # This enables building divisible chains using dynamic programming from smaller to larger values.\n    # Python's sorted is stable, preserving original relative order for equal absolute values.\n    sorted_nums = sorted(nums, key=abs)\n\n    # dp[i] will store the length of the longest divisible subset ending with sorted_nums[i].\n    dp = [1] * n\n\n    for i in range(n):\n        val_i = sorted_nums[i]\n        for j in range(i):\n            val_j = sorted_nums[j]\n\n            # Check for divisibility following the rule: \"0 is only divisible with equal zeros\".\n            # A chain element val_i can follow val_j if val_i is divisible by val_j.\n            is_divisible = False\n            if val_j == 0:\n                # If the predecessor (val_j) is zero, the successor (val_i) must also be zero\n                # to form a divisible pair according to the rule.\n                if val_i == 0:\n                    is_divisible = True\n            elif val_i != 0:\n                # If predecessor (val_j) is not zero, and successor (val_i) is also not zero,\n                # check divisibility using absolute values.\n                if abs(val_i) % abs(val_j) == 0:\n                    is_divisible = True\n            # If val_j is non-zero and val_i is zero, they are not divisible by the rule.\n\n            if is_divisible:\n                if dp[j] + 1 > dp[i]:\n                    dp[i] = dp[j] + 1\n    \n    # The maximum value in the dp array is the size of the largest divisible subset.\n    return max(dp)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/626", "solution": "def triangle_area(radius):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the area of the largest triangle that can be inscribed in a semicircle.\n    Returns the area (radius squared) for a positive radius, or None for an invalid radius.\n    \"\"\"\n    try:\n        r = float(radius)\n    except (TypeError, ValueError):\n        return None\n\n    if not (r > 0):\n        return None\n\n    return r * r"}
